    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d903">

                <pb facs="0760" n="752" sameAs="frus:frus1937v04/pg752"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">711.008 North Pacific/137</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to
                        the Ambassador in Japan</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >Grew</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <seg rendition="#float-left">No. 1305</seg>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1937-07-23">July 23, 1937</date>.</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sir</hi>: There is enclosed for your information and
                    consideration a copy of a letter under date July 9, 1937, from Senator Homer T.
                        Bone,<anchor n="57" xml:id="d903-f1"/> suggesting that this Government
                    should obtain full information about the destination of all Japanese fishing
                    vessels, where they fish and what they catch, and also that permission be
                    obtained from the Japanese Government for representatives of this Government to
                    board Japanese public and private vessels in Bering Sea and examine their
                    operations. The Department’s reply is also enclosed.<anchor n="57"
                        xml:id="d903-f2"/> The Department <pb facs="0761" n="753" xml:id="pg753"/>
                    will give consideration to any recommendations which may occur to you with
                    reference to Senator Bone’s suggestions.</p>
                <p>Very truly yours,</p>
                <closer>For the Secretary of State:<lb/><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Sumner
                            Welles</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
